================================================================================
SPAN — Swift Parameter-free Attention Network for Efficient Super-Resolution
================================================================================

    Licence:  Apache License, Version 2.0
    Source:   https://github.com/hongyuanyu/SPAN
    Paper:    Wan et al., "Swift Parameter-free Attention Network for Efficient
              Super-Resolution", CVPR Workshops 2024

Lucid's upscaler is derived from the SPAN architecture. It is not the published
network and it does not use the published weights:

  * The trunk is rearranged. A PixelUnshuffle(2) is placed before it so the 18
    trunk convolutions run at quarter area and the reconstruction head does x8
    instead of x4. See Tools/train_span.py, class `Unshuffled`.
  * `img_range` is 1 and the RGB mean is zero, rather than the stock 255 scaling.
  * The weights are trained from scratch by this project on a corpus of real
    codec degradation (libx264 and libvpx-vp9 at 90 kbps to 1.5 Mbps, GOP 30 to
    250), not on bicubic downsampling. No published SPAN checkpoint is used.

The Core ML packages under Model/SPAN_x4_ch32u_*.mlpackage that ship inside
Lucid.app are the output of that training, converted for the Neural Engine.
Because they are produced by code derived from SPAN, they are covered by this
attribution.

A copy of the Apache License, Version 2.0 is available at:

    http://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0

The upstream SPAN checkout, when present, lives at Model/SPAN and carries its
own LICENSE and LICENSE/ directory. It is not tracked in this repository and is
not redistributed here; Tools/convert_span.py loads the architecture from it.
That checkout also vendors licences for pytorch-image-models, SwinIR,
stylegan2-pytorch and an NVIDIA source file, which apply to the parts of BasicSR
those came from and not to anything Lucid ships.


================================================================================
Algorithms implemented from published descriptions
================================================================================

The filters in Lucid/Metal/DetailEnhancer.swift are written here from the
published descriptions of the following work. No code was copied from these
projects, and they are named because the ideas are theirs:

  * CDEF, the constrained directional enhancement filter, from AV1
    — Steinar Midtskogen and Jean-Marc Valin. The per-direction divisor table
      matches the one used by dav1d.
  * Temporal accumulation with neighbourhood clipping
    — Brian Karis (temporal anti-aliasing) and Playdead's shipped implementation.
  * Stochastic debanding
    — the approach used by libplacebo and mpv.
  * Contrast-adaptive sharpening
    — the lobe from AMD FidelityFX CAS.
  * Oklab
    — Bjorn Ottosson.
  * The deblocking filter
    — ITU-T Recommendation H.264, Tables 8-16 and 8-17, run without a bitstream.


